Q: Reason for timeout when curly brackets are used in expect command A: <p>In <code>expect</code> scripts, the default behavior of the <code>expect</code> command if it doesn't have a custom timeout handler and a timeout happens before a match is to just return. That's almost certainly the case in your first example - the regular expression isn't matching any more than it is in your second one. I suspect that if you change the timeout to -1 to make it infinite, the first script will hang and never get around to <code>send</code>ing anything.</p>
<p>The reason for this is the <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> anchors - by default in <code>tcl</code> regexps, they don't match beginning and end of <em>lines</em>, they match the beginning and end of the entirety of the input, which likely (I don't have access to any Cisco hardware to confirm for sure) has more than just 'Password:' in it. That's why you usually will see things like</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>expect { 
        Password: { send -- &quot;admin\r&quot; }
        timeout   { puts &quot;took too much time&quot;; exit }
        eof       { puts &quot;finished in time&quot; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>which uses unanchored wildcard glob matching; you can also use <code>-ex Password:</code> for exact unanchored string matching. If you insist on using a regular expression (Though it's not needed in this trivial case), there's two things to change:</p>
<p>First, turn on <a href="https://www.tcl-lang.org/man/tcl8.6.17/TclCmd/re_syntax.htm#M95" rel="nofollow noreferrer">newline-sensitive matching</a> with a <code>(?n)</code> <a href="https://www.tcl-lang.org/man/tcl8.6.17/TclCmd/re_syntax.htm#M82" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><em>embedded option</em></a> at the beginning of the pattern.</p>
<p>Demonstration of how it changes matching:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>% regexp {^Password:$} &quot;Welcome to Cisco!\nPassword:\n&quot;
0
% regexp {(?n)^Password:$} &quot;Welcome to Cisco!\nPassword:\n&quot;
1
</code></pre>
<p>Second, because <code>expect</code> forces CRLF line endings and <code>$</code> in newline-sensitive mode matches before the linefeed, you need to allow for that carriage return. <code>-re {(?n)^Password:(?:\r?)$}</code> should result in a match.</p>
